const object = `{
  "material": "ceramic",
  "tags": ["blue", "sale"],
  "capacity_ml": 350,
  "offers": [{"seller":"A","price":12}, {"seller":"B","price":18}]
}`;
const typed = `ALTER TABLE pg_cap_products ADD CONSTRAINT numeric_capacity
CHECK (
  NOT (attributes ? 'capacity_ml')
  OR jsonb_typeof(attributes->'capacity_ml') = 'number'
);`;
const repair = `UPDATE pg_cap_products
SET attributes = jsonb_set(attributes, '{capacity_ml}', '350'::jsonb)
WHERE product_id = 4
RETURNING attributes->'capacity_ml' AS repaired_capacity;`;
const patch = `UPDATE pg_cap_products
SET attributes = jsonb_set(attributes, '{material}', '"stoneware"')
WHERE product_id = 1
RETURNING attributes;`;
---
<Base title="JSON queries in PostgreSQL" description="Follow JSON objects through containment, correlated array predicates, SQL/JSON rows and indexes that match the searched expression.">
<article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">PostgreSQL</p><h1>JSON queries in PostgreSQL</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>PostgreSQL 18</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/tables-and-documents/">Tables and JSON ↗</a></div></header>
 <p>A JSON value has structure inside a column: named fields, nested objects and arrays. PostgreSQL can ask questions about that structure without turning every field into a separate column. It can also index selected parts of it. The interesting choice is which structure the query needs to preserve, and which information an index must keep to answer it.</p>
 <p>“An offer from seller A costs less than 15” sounds close to “there is an offer from A, and there is an offer costing less than 15.” They can produce different products. Likewise, indexing the containing JSON object does not make every expression extracted from that object an indexed lookup. We will work through both distinctions, then give a numeric attribute an explicit type policy.</p>
 <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/tables-and-documents/">representation article</a> develops the choice between related records and embedded attributes. Here we keep a chosen JSON representation long enough to understand its queries and access paths.</p>
 <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#objects">Objects and containment</a></li><li><a href="#offers">Matching conditions within one array element</a></li><li><a href="#rows">JSON as query rows</a></li><li><a href="#indexes">GIN indexes on JSON expressions</a></li><li><a href="#types">Validating and indexing numeric attributes</a></li><li><a href="#writes">Changing a document</a></li></ol></nav>

 <section id="objects"><h2>Objects and containment</h2>
 <p>Our products have an integer <code>product_id</code>, a name and an <code>attributes jsonb</code> column. The ID and name remain ordinary relational columns. PostgreSQL stores <code>jsonb</code> as a parsed representation, so operators can navigate its fields. It does not preserve the original whitespace, key order or repeated object keys. Retaining an exact incoming message would be a different requirement from retaining these product facts.</p>
 <p>Product 1, the blue mug, has this object. Prices use the same currency throughout the example.</p>
 <Sql code={object} label="Product 1's attributes" />
 <p>Three more records give us useful differences. Product 2 swaps the sellers' prices. Product 3 is a steel flask. Product 4 came from an import that encoded capacity as a string; it still has valid JSON.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Four products and their JSON fields"><table><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Material / tags</th><th>Capacity</th><th>Offers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 · Blue mug</td><td>ceramic / blue, sale</td><td>350, number</td><td>A: 12; B: 18</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Red mug</td><td>ceramic / red</td><td>250, number</td><td>A: 18; B: 12</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Blue flask</td><td>steel / blue</td><td>500, number</td><td>Empty array</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Imported mug</td><td>ceramic / blue</td><td>“350”, string</td><td>Empty array</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>In the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-json-queries">JSON query lesson</a>, run the setup once, then send the following queries from the same editor. The four products remain available between runs. A separate generated table is reserved for index experiments; it does not change these four records.</p>
 <Sql code={sql.containment} label="Find ceramic products with a blue tag" />
 <p><code>@&gt;</code> asks whether the left object contains the structure on the right. Products 1 and 4 match. Product 1's extra sale tag and offers do not disqualify it; we did not ask for an equal whole object. Product 3 has blue but the wrong material. Product 4's string capacity does not matter to this predicate because capacity is absent from the requested structure.</p>
 <p>Array containment is not an ordered-sequence test. Reversing blue and sale inside product 1's tag array does not change its membership. If the order of steps in a workflow is meaningful, use a query that inspects that order rather than borrowing this tag test.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="offers"><h2>Matching conditions within one array element</h2>
 <p>We now want an offer from A below 15. Product 1 qualifies: its A offer costs 12. Product 2 does not: A charges 18, even though B charges 12. The test must keep seller and price attached to the same array member.</p>
 <Sql code={sql.correlated} label="Compare independent existence tests with one offer filter" />
 <p><code>@?</code> checks whether a JSON path produces a matching item. The path starts at the document root, <code>$</code>; <code>.offers[*]</code> visits its offers one at a time. Inside the filter, <code>@</code> names the offer currently being considered. In <code>same_offer</code>, both conditions examine that one offer before the path moves to another.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Offer predicate results"><table><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Separate tests</th><th>Same offer</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>true</td><td>true</td><td>A itself charges 12</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>true</td><td>false</td><td>A exists; the cheap offer belongs to B</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>The independent version throws away the relationship before combining its answers. Each path returns only the fact that some member matched. Its final <code>AND</code> cannot recover whether they were the same member. This is the same reasoning needed when joining related rows: matching facts must remain associated with the right identity.</p>
 <p>Change the limit from 15 to 20 and both A offers qualify. That variation changes the business question, not the meaning of the path. PostgreSQL's <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-json.html">JSON path operators</a> also suppress several structural and type errors. A search over inconsistent objects can therefore simply omit unsuitable items. That behaviour is useful for discovery but does not validate the documents on the way in.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="rows"><h2>JSON as query rows</h2>
 <p>A path is convenient for asking whether an offer exists. A report that needs seller names, amounts and a join to a seller table benefits from rows. <code>JSON_TABLE</code>, available since PostgreSQL 17, supplies rows to a query without first creating a stored table.</p>
 <Sql code={sql.rows} label="Expand offers and filter each resulting row" />
 <p>For each product, the expression reads that product's attributes. Each matching offer becomes one row with a text seller and numeric price. There are four offer rows before filtering: 1/A/12, 1/B/18, 2/A/18 and 2/B/12. Products 3 and 4 contribute none because their arrays are empty. The filter leaves 1/A/12.</p>
 <p>The parent ID travels beside the expanded fields, so a later join can identify both the product and its seller. <code>ERROR ON ERROR</code> makes a price-conversion failure fail this query. It does not make price required: missing values follow the column's empty-result policy, whose default is null. Choose those policies deliberately if supplier input varies. A conversion that accepts a numeric string may be appropriate for ingestion, but is different from requiring JSON numbers.</p>
 <p>Expansion also changes cardinality. Joining all four offers to the product records repeats products 1 and 2 twice. Counting the expanded rows counts offers, not products. If each offer later needs independent identity, references, history or frequent updates, a real related table may be the clearer representation. Query-time expansion does not provide those stored invariants.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="indexes"><h2>GIN indexes on JSON expressions</h2>
 <p>Finding a value inside one small object is different from locating the matching objects among millions. GIN maintains searchable entries extracted from indexed values and references to candidate rows. An expression index can choose a smaller part of each value to supply those entries.</p>
 <JsonPath />
 <p>The larger fixture has 10,000 objects. Every twentieth product has a blue tag; the other 9,500 have red. First we index the complete attributes value, then search an extracted tags array:</p>
 <Sql code={sql.wholeIndex} label="A whole-object GIN beside a query on extracted tags" />
 <p>The checked PostgreSQL 18.6 plan scans the table, emits 500 rows and removes 9,500. The <code>?</code> operator is applied to <code>attributes-&gt;'tags'</code>, not to the value stored in this index. GIN's support for JSON existence does not make those two expressions identical. A whole-object containment query can use the whole-object index; this query needs a matching expression index.</p>
 <Sql code={sql.targetedIndex} label="Index the array that the predicate searches" />
 <p>The same query now uses <code>pg_cap_json_tags</code> in a bitmap index scan. The bitmap heap scan visits candidate heap pages and returns the same 500 products. It is the answer-preserving access path that changed. Editing blue to red makes 9,500 rows qualify; when a query needs most of the table, following an index can be less attractive than scanning it. Inspect the resulting plan instead of requiring an index node as evidence of correctness.</p>
 <p>Operator classes further define what GIN extracts and which predicates it can support. The default <code>jsonb_ops</code> supports key existence as well as containment. <code>jsonb_path_ops</code> supports containment and path matching, but not <code>?</code>. Choosing it for this tags index would remove the operation we need. Its smaller, more specific entries can help other workloads; the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/datatype-json.html#JSON-INDEXING">JSON indexing documentation</a> explains the distinction.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="types"><h2>Validating and indexing numeric attributes</h2>
 <p>Capacity introduces a different question: products holding at least a certain number of millilitres. GIN membership does not arrange capacities in numeric order. Before adding a B-tree, we need to decide what value the expression should produce when a supplier sends the string “350.”</p>
 <Sql code={sql.numeric} label="Extract only JSON numbers as numeric values" />
 <p>The results are 350, 250, 500 and SQL null. The <code>CASE</code> reaches the cast only for JSON numbers, so the string does not silently become an accepted measurement. A later numeric comparison excludes that null. This is a tolerant read policy: the bad representation remains in storage, visible for an import-repair report.</p>
 <p>The final lesson step indexes this same guarded expression on the larger fixture. Its capacities cycle from 100 through 599 twenty times. A range of 590 or more therefore has 200 matches. The checked plan uses the expression B-tree. Keeping the guard identical in the query and index makes both the type policy and access path explicit.</p>
 <Sql code={sql.rangeIndex} label="An ordered index for the guarded numeric expression" />
 <p>If the application requires every present capacity to be a JSON number, enforce that policy on writes instead. The following constraint would reject our current table because product 4 is already inconsistent:</p>
 <Sql code={typed} label="A write invariant, to add only after repairing existing input" />
 <p>For this known import record, we have checked that the source measurement was numeric 350 millilitres. Correct its representation, then send the constraint statement above. It now succeeds.</p>
 <Sql code={repair} label="Repair the confirmed measurement before enforcing the rule" />
 <p>This version permits a missing capacity but rejects a present string or JSON null. Requiring every product to have a positive integer capacity would be a different rule, and is probably wrong for products that are not containers. A dedicated typed details row can express that narrower business category without making every object follow the mug schema.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="writes"><h2>Changing a document</h2>
 <p>An index is maintained when its indexed value changes. Adding whole-object GIN, tag GIN and a capacity B-tree to every product is therefore a write and storage decision, not a free collection of future options. Start with the queries that justify them, and measure the read/write workload together.</p>
 <p>A writer can change one key relative to the currently stored object:</p>
 <Sql code={patch} label="Patch material while retaining the other stored fields" />
 <p>This retains the blue and sale tags, capacity and offers. Sending an entire object assembled from an earlier read could instead overwrite somebody else's intervening changes. The patch still updates the containing row and takes its row lock; two writers changing different keys do not acquire independent key locks.</p>
 <p>Large JSON values also participate in PostgreSQL's ordinary variable-length storage. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-toast.html">TOAST</a> can compress values or move them out of the main tuple. A small path change does not turn that value into independently stored, independently locked business records. If unrelated sections are large and frequently edited, split them according to ownership and update needs, then keep the JSON where it remains a coherent value.</p>
 <p>The result is a more specific design than “use JSON and add an index.” The offers predicate preserves one seller-price pair, the tags index extracts the searched array, the capacity expression carries a type policy, and the writer changes the current stored value. Each choice follows from a question we can now test.</p>
 </section>
